/*
 * dist/libxtc.map -- ld version script for the shared libxtc.so.
 *
 * Copyright (c) 2026, The XTC Project
 * Use of this source code is governed by the ISC License.
 *
 * This script controls which symbols the shared library exports.
 * It is passed to the linker via -Wl,--version-script=libxtc.map
 * when configure was run with --enable-shared.
 *
 * Export policy (matches test/m0/test_symbols.sh [C5]/[C7]):
 *   global:  xtc_*    -- the documented public API.
 *            __xtc_proc_recovery_slot, __xtc_recovery_prep,
 *            __xtc_recovery_ctx, __xtc_recovery_result -- the ONLY
 *            internal symbols a PUBLIC macro in an installed header
 *            (xtc_proc_recovery_arm() in src/inc/xtc_proc.h) expands
 *            to, so they must stay callable from inlined consumer
 *            code.  (POSIX uses __xtc_proc_recovery_slot via
 *            sigsetjmp; the other three are the Windows RtlCaptureContext
 *            path.  They are matched by the __xtc_recovery_* pattern, not
 *            named: lld -- FreeBSD's default linker -- rejects a version
 *            script that names an undefined symbol ("version script
 *            assignment ... failed: symbol not defined"), and those
 *            three are never defined in an ELF build; a pattern that
 *            matches nothing is not an error.  The pattern is exactly
 *            the three Windows names' prefix, so it cannot widen the
 *            ELF export set.)
 *   local:   EVERYTHING else -- the whole __os_* OS-abstraction layer
 *            AND every other __xtc_* internal (sim hooks, the
 *            async-signal-unsafe bracket, tail hook points, fcontext
 *            seams, ...).  None of these is public API, so none leaks
 *            into the shared-library ABI.
 *
 * This is an explicit allowlist, NOT a "__xtc_* glob": after internal
 * __ decls were moved out of the installed headers (see the *_int.h
 * split + the [C7] gate), a broad __xtc_* export would leak ~80 pure
 * internals.  Keep this list in sync with the macro-backed exception
 * the [C7] gate allows -- test/m0/test_symbols.sh [C8] asserts the two
 * agree.
 *
 * Symbol versioning (2.0).  Every exported symbol carries the version
 * node XTC_2.0 (`nm -D` shows xtc_foo@@XTC_2.0), so a consumer linked
 * against 2.0 records the node it needs and the dynamic loader refuses
 * an older library that lacks it ("version `XTC_2.1' not found") instead
 * of failing at the first missing symbol.  The node is matched by glob
 * (xtc_*), so a new function is versioned automatically -- which is
 * exactly why the policy below needs a human:
 *
 * POLICY for later releases (docs/abi-stability.md "Symbol versions"):
 *   - A MINOR release N that ADDS functions lists them by name in a new
 *     node `XTC_2.N { global: xtc_new_a; xtc_new_b; } XTC_2.(N-1);`
 *     placed ABOVE the XTC_2.0 node (an explicit name binds before a
 *     glob), so they carry @@XTC_2.N and a consumer that uses them
 *     cannot load against a 2.(N-1) library.
 *   - Symbols are never moved out of the node they shipped in, and a
 *     node is never edited after its release.  A changed signature is a
 *     NEW function name (or a MAJOR).
 *   - A MAJOR release starts over with a single XTC_<MAJOR>.0 node and a
 *     new SONAME (libxtc.so.MAJOR); test/m0/test_symbols.sh [C9] checks
 *     that the base node matches the MAJOR in dist/version.in.
 * The Mach-O (libxtc.exp) and Windows builds have no symbol versions;
 * they get the same export SET (see [C8]).
 */
XTC_2.0 {
	global:
		xtc_*;
		__xtc_proc_recovery_slot;
		__xtc_recovery_*;
	local:
		*;
};
